+::a27b38c1a1 Currently there are the following weak points:
+
+### ::a27b38c1a2 Usability
+
+- ::a27b38c1a3 Checking the fingerprints and signatures of the commits currently needs some technical expertise (e.g. running shell commands). ::a27b38c1a4 This could be simplified with dedicated software but that software should be managed or at least audited by an independent entity. ::a27b38c1a5 A verification tool controlled by the same instance it should verify is pointless.
+
+### ::a27b38c1a6 Actual integrity
+
+- ::a27b38c1a7 The bad actor scenario here is a denial rather than an attack. ::a27b38c1a8 Faced with two signed versions of the same history, the platform could claim that its signing key had been stolen and that one of those signatures is therefore not its own. ::a27b38c1a9 Nothing inside the repository refutes that on its own. ::a27b38c1a10 What would help is a copy of the repository kept by an independent and trustworthy third party, and that is not implemented yet. ::a27b38c1a11 The risk itself is small, since a signing key leaving the platform is an unlikely event. ::a27b38c1a12 Key changes may still become necessary for ordinary reasons, and those are harmless. ::a27b38c1a13 As long as a key change does not come together with a conflict in the commit history, there is nothing to explain.
+- ::a27b38c1a14 There will be situations when changing the commit history is necessary. ::a27b38c1a15 E.g. this explanatory repo has to be updated along with the software, or unlawful contributions have to be removed from the platform. ::a27b38c1a16 Currently a clean way to handle and document such cases is not yet implemented.
+- ::a27b38c1a17 The debate-integrity-concept and its implementation have not yet been audited by independent experts. ::a27b38c1a18 It is thus not unlikely that Fair Debate is affected by security flaws. ::a27b38c1a19 But this is a general problem with software and being aware of it usually is an important step in preventing critical incidents.
+- ::a27b38c1a20 A fingerprint shows that a text has not changed since it was published. ::a27b38c1a21 It does not show who wrote it. ::a27b38c1a22 The commits are created and signed by this platform, not by the authors, so the platform could in principle put a contribution under somebody's name. ::a27b38c1a23 Signatures by the contributors themselves would close that gap and do not exist here yet.
+- ::a27b38c1a24 The platform could show one history to one reader and a different one to another, and sign both. ::a27b38c1a25 That is only noticed when the two readers compare, which is precisely what a public mirror of the repositories would automate. ::a27b38c1a26 There is none yet.
+- ::a27b38c1a27 Whoever controls the server controls the signing key, the administrators of the data centre included. ::a27b38c1a28 That risk comes with any IT infrastructure. ::a27b38c1a29 What malicious actors, however, cannot do is take back the signatures already handed out. ::a27b38c1a30 A manipulation therefore stays detectable and can be resolved.
